WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.190
- Welcome, everyone, to
CIFAR's series of virtual talks

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:07.810
on the theme Science as the Exit Strategy.

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:10.650
These talks are intended
to share how research

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:12.020
from across the academy,

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:15.080
from computer science,
population and public health

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:18.790
to economics and
anthropology form the basis

00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:21.090
for evidence-informed decision making

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:23.710
as governments begin
planning how best to reopen

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:27.793
shuttered societies and economies
in the wake of COVID-19.

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:31.010
If you're not familiar with us,

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:34.600
CIFAR is a Canadian-based
global charitable organization

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.640
that convenes extraordinary minds

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.680
to address some of science
and humanity's most important

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:41.163
and pressing questions.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:44.970
Like many other organizations
across the globe,

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:49.730
CIFAR has pivoted quickly
to take action on COVID-19,

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:51.950
launching new projects and initiatives,

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:53.910
some of which we are highlighting

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:55.663
through this webinar series.

00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:58.640
My name is Kate Geddie.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:01.300
I am senior director of research at CIFAR,

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:04.350
and it's my true pleasure
to introduce you today

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:07.180
to Professor Hendrik Poinar.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:09.920
Hendrik is professor of anthropology

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.490
at McMaster University,

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:13.730
where he draws on his training

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:16.640
in molecular evolutionary genetics

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:18.700
and biological anthropology

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:22.120
to address questions about past pandemics.

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:25.850
In particular, he uses chemical
and molecular techniques

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:29.990
within forensic, archeological,
and paleontological remains

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:32.580
to ask questions such as

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:36.460
which pathogens are
responsible for past pandemics?

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:39.010
He's also a fellow in CIFAR's

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:42.330
Humans and the Microbiome program,

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:44.180
and he'll be sharing with us today

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:46.220
how knowledge of past pandemics

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:50.950
can inform our understanding
of the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:54.113
So, Hendrik, to start
off our conversation,

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:57.790
I'd like to ask you about the broad themes

00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:01.440
that emerge in art
depicting past epidemics.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:05.110
So, for example, looking at this painting

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:06.680
by Antoine Jean Gros,

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:09.240
hopefully that'll be coming up, yep,

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.160
that centers on Napoleon,

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:13.390
what role does historical art play

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:16.180
in how we view and think about pandemics?

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:18.300
- Oh, so first, thanks for having me.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:21.060
It's a pleasure and
honor to be part of this

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.380
talking point today,

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:25.060
and welcome to everybody who's listening.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:26.336
Yeah, I mean, this is a great painting.

00:02:26.336 --> 00:02:28.540
Art has always been a powerful way

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:31.160
for people to reflect
and comment on pandemics,

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:33.573
socially, politically, and individually.

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:37.980
This, it's a perfect tool,
art, to illustrate both human

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:40.320
and political responses
to pandemics in the past

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:42.640
and, probably not
surprisingly to most of you,

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:45.030
is tremendously relevant today.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:47.000
There's lots of important points in this.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.800
We could talk about this
painting for hours and hours,

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.620
but I'll try to minimize those to a few

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:53.270
of the important points.

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:55.500
So, first, if we put it into context,

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:59.150
this is painted by
Antoine Jean Gros in 1804

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:01.167
and is titled "Napoleon Bonaparte Visiting

00:03:01.167 --> 00:03:02.943
"the Plague-Stricken at Jaffa",

00:03:02.943 --> 00:03:05.110
and this was part of
Napoleon's Egyptian campaign

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:09.303
to fight the Ottomans as he
moved across Egypt and Syria.

00:03:10.167 --> 00:03:12.340
And this takes place basically

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:17.340
after the brutal siege of Jaffa in 1799,

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:20.630
and shortly thereafter,

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:23.450
there was an outbreak of bubonic plague.

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:26.100
And most of you will know, of course,

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.513
that plagues and war go hand-in-hand.

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:31.590
And so if we thing about plague

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:33.010
and sort of bubonic plague,

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:35.750
an 18th century plague epidemic,

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:37.870
this is one of the last ones recorded

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:40.650
in the Middle East and Europe.

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:43.420
This is a descendant, this epidemic,

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:46.740
of the famous Black Death
which happened in 1346

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:49.860
and spread across Europe
killing about 50 million people.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:51.110
But I think importantly

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:53.690
that that number seems out of context

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:55.810
unless you place it into
the overall mortality rate,

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:58.480
which is something between 30 and 40%

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:01.020
of the total population which is...

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:02.956
We think about the
numbers today with COVID,

00:04:02.956 --> 00:04:06.200
and they are dramatic nonetheless,

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:09.663
but I think in terms of the
context of the total population,

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:13.900
it was a tremendous wipe,
of course, to the Europeans.

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:18.900
So, here, Napoleon is visiting
his plague-stricken soldiers,

00:04:21.490 --> 00:04:23.330
and he sits, I think what's so interesting

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:25.520
is that there's really
two important points

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:27.140
that I wanna illustrate in this painting.

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:29.660
One, of course, is he sits
dead center of this painting,

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:31.730
and of course, we'll talk about, really,

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:34.530
the meaning of that in a second.

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:36.830
The light's hitting him from the top.

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:39.424
And of course, it's drawn at the time

00:04:39.424 --> 00:04:42.590
in an Oriental framework,

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:45.670
which was a very important
part of European life.

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:49.520
There's sort of the mystery
of the Orient at that time.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:52.460
So, I think the most important
narrative that it draws upon

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:54.360
is this idea of use versus them.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:57.960
This is a typical narrative of epidemics.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:04:59.860
We always think about people,

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:01.740
those that are sick and
those that are healthy.

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:03.810
But this case, of course,
there's a double entendre

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:05.170
because those that are sick,

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:07.310
and you can see at the
center of the painting

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:09.010
Napoleon touching a sick soldier,

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:11.973
actually touching a bubo
of the sick soldier.

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:16.820
These soldiers are supposed
to be his French soldiers,

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:19.310
but they look very similar to the soldiers

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:21.710
at the very front of the painting

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:23.420
in the dark sort of shadows,

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:26.080
which are also Arab
soldiers that are dying,

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:28.650
Ottoman soldiers that are
dying from the plague as well.

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:31.220
And I think here you have the balance

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:32.850
of the sick versus the healthy,

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:35.040
but you also have this narrative of

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:37.970
the disease came from
afar, came from away.

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:40.180
And it describes this idea

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:42.293
of what we call European exceptionalism,

00:05:42.293 --> 00:05:46.240
that the Europeans could never
themselves harbor disease.

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:48.770
It was always diseases
coming from the East.

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:52.580
And it's probably strange or maybe not

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:55.000
that we still have that narrative
playing out today, right?

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:59.100
COVID is a Chinese disease
or a Chinese virus,

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:01.650
and that's no different
than what we saw here.

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:05.480
In fact, our work, actually,
on the DNA of plague victims

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:08.080
throughout Europe throughout
the second pandemic

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:09.950
show that, actually,
that's not at all true.

00:06:09.950 --> 00:06:12.670
Plague actually maintained
itself in local reservoirs

00:06:12.670 --> 00:06:14.030
and kept emerging,

00:06:14.030 --> 00:06:17.240
so plague was a very European disease

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:19.450
even though the Europeans
like to think of themselves

00:06:19.450 --> 00:06:21.860
as being exceptional.

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:24.140
And so in this case, the
disease is coming from the East.

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:26.140
It's coming from people that don't know

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:28.920
how to care for themselves
and take care for themselves

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:30.010
and so on and so forth,

00:06:30.010 --> 00:06:32.450
and so I think that's one
of the important narratives,

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:33.970
us versus them.

00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:36.300
The second thing that I think is probably

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:38.110
extremely relevant today is that,

00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:40.630
remember that this is a propaganda piece.

00:06:40.630 --> 00:06:43.610
This was commissioned by Napoleon in 1804

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:46.730
to be a piece that would allow him

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:49.010
to rise as emperor, right?

00:06:49.010 --> 00:06:50.380
He wasn't emperor at this point,

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:51.580
but he was hoping that this piece

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:53.320
would sort of help illuminate

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:55.543
several of his important points.

00:06:56.950 --> 00:06:58.500
Napoleon sits dead center.

00:06:58.500 --> 00:06:59.940
We have the light, basically,

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:02.780
from the heavens highlighting Napoleon,

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:05.430
and he's doing what we know

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:07.910
from the Bible, of course,
Christ did with lepers

00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:11.810
or the king during medieval
times would do with sick people,

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:15.050
which is basically laying
the hands upon the sick

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:17.050
in the attempts to heal them.

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:20.380
So, but it's also to illustrate
to the people in France

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:22.980
that Napoleon was tremendously brave,

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:25.390
that he would unglove himself.

00:07:25.390 --> 00:07:27.470
I mean, how important are gloves

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:29.300
in the current pandemic,
what we think about,

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:30.910
but he's taking off his glove

00:07:30.910 --> 00:07:33.120
and he's actually touching the bubo.

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:36.320
And if we can just zoom
in on that illustration

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:39.140
in the next image,

00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:43.890
can see that not only is he touching that,

00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:45.120
but look at his officers

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:47.970
and his doctors who are
both behind him, right?

00:07:47.970 --> 00:07:50.993
They're horrified, which is
the true representation of,

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:53.260
as the painter knew,

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:56.600
people were scared out of
their minds about plague

00:07:56.600 --> 00:07:59.830
because plague had
repeatedly erupted in Europe

00:07:59.830 --> 00:08:03.630
for 400 years killing millions
and millions of people.

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:05.280
And so this doctor behind him

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:07.933
is not surprisingly donning a mask.

00:08:08.950 --> 00:08:10.610
And of course, that's a narrative theme

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:13.600
that we have repeatedly introduced today

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:16.080
and actually, unfortunately,
back and forth, right?

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:17.747
It should've been wearing
masks from the beginning,

00:08:17.747 --> 00:08:19.660
and of course, we got conflicting messages

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:21.500
about how useful those would be.

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:23.097
And so the doctor behind him is saying,

00:08:23.097 --> 00:08:25.210
"Hey, Napoleon, you're
nuts, this is crazy."

00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:27.940
But of course, as part
of the propaganda piece,

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:30.620
it gives legitimacy,
of course, to the power

00:08:31.630 --> 00:08:34.950
and to his domineering role,

00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:38.110
that he has this heavenly, God-like power

00:08:38.110 --> 00:08:41.900
that he can touch the
sick, hopefully heal them,

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:45.263
and therefore maintain a
level above the common people.

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:49.070
So, this power illustrated
in this illustration

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:52.003
is really a legitimacy to continue ruling.

00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:57.630
- Well, that's really
interesting and that,

00:08:57.630 --> 00:09:00.400
as you've alluded to
in your response there,

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:04.290
some of our current practices
in response to COVID-19

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:05.390
also are not new.

00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:07.520
I mean, you've talked about
the wearing of the mask,

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:10.040
but also maybe if we go to another image

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:13.850
that we have here from the 1918 flu,

00:09:13.850 --> 00:09:18.220
we can see that the sick are
often isolated from the healthy

00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:20.760
just as we're attempting to quarantine

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:23.420
those sick with the coronavirus.

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:26.660
So, I mean, what do our responses

00:09:26.660 --> 00:09:28.210
tell us about the relationship between,

00:09:28.210 --> 00:09:30.230
on the one hand, scientific understandings

00:09:30.230 --> 00:09:33.927
and social practices such as
stigmatization on the other?

00:09:35.380 --> 00:09:38.790
- Yeah, they're two very important points.

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:41.180
Yeah, I mean, this is Camp Funston, Kansas

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:44.390
with the American soldiers
that are sick of influenza,

00:09:44.390 --> 00:09:46.650
and of course, the scientific
reason for quarantine,

00:09:46.650 --> 00:09:49.400
of course, is to stem or
quell the spread of infection.

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:51.380
And this, of course, comes from,

00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:53.360
actually, the earliest
known quarantine examples

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:55.203
come from the city of Ragusa,

00:09:56.720 --> 00:09:58.470
which, of course, is modern day Dubrovnik,

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:01.030
and the Venetians closely thereafter

00:10:01.030 --> 00:10:04.100
took it as quaranta, quarantine,

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:05.870
40 days of keeping a ship

00:10:05.870 --> 00:10:08.120
on these small islands
off the coast of Venice

00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:09.820
in the hopes of quelling the disease.

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:12.110
But of course, you think
about that 40 days on a ship,

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:14.770
and yet every time I think about quaranta,

00:10:14.770 --> 00:10:17.750
I think about the poor
people on the cruise ships

00:10:17.750 --> 00:10:19.980
and thinking how uncomfortable this is.

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:21.780
I mean, so the scientific
reason, of course,

00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:24.503
is the hope to keep the disease at bay,

00:10:25.550 --> 00:10:26.520
but of course, there's political

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:28.810
and social motivation for doing this.

00:10:28.810 --> 00:10:33.150
So, this is the 1918 influenza epidemic,

00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:34.860
and no government at the time

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:37.530
wanted to acknowledge its
presence, right, on their soil.

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:40.240
In fact, all Western journalists in Europe

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:43.320
and in North America were banned and fined

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:46.050
and threatened with prison
time if they reported on it.

00:10:46.050 --> 00:10:47.530
And there's only one country

00:10:47.530 --> 00:10:49.200
that actually reported freely on it,

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:50.360
and of course, that was Spain,

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:52.003
hence the name Spanish Flu.

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:55.140
And so this just illustrates, again,

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:59.440
that the quarantine serves
a political purpose,

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:02.090
which is to make the sick

00:11:03.330 --> 00:11:05.370
not visible to the rest of us.

00:11:05.370 --> 00:11:08.640
And so quarantine,
actually, we try to frame,

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:10.930
governments will try to frame quarantine

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:14.880
as being an important
part to protect everybody

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:16.120
and to help those that are sick,

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:17.030
but of course, it never is.

00:11:17.030 --> 00:11:18.380
It's never for those who need it the most,

00:11:18.380 --> 00:11:19.213
which are the sick.

00:11:19.213 --> 00:11:23.830
It's always a way to
protect the population

00:11:23.830 --> 00:11:25.447
from those that are sick, right?

00:11:25.447 --> 00:11:27.980
And so maybe another way to look at that

00:11:27.980 --> 00:11:30.310
is the next illustration, which is...

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:36.726
Yeah, this is the TB
patients in a sanatorium.

00:11:36.726 --> 00:11:40.100
And of course, many of you probably know

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:42.460
of a sanatorium just in your backyards.

00:11:42.460 --> 00:11:43.720
I mean, there's one here in Hamilton

00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:46.420
and there's several across
Toronto and upstate New York.

00:11:46.420 --> 00:11:48.343
And these were TB patients.

00:11:49.670 --> 00:11:52.580
And they were sent to
sanatorium not out of the,

00:11:52.580 --> 00:11:55.320
what I like to call the
Thomas Mann sort of romantic,

00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:58.030
alpine vision of oh, it's
gonna be beautiful days

00:11:58.030 --> 00:12:01.330
looking at these edelweiss
in the Swiss Alps,

00:12:01.330 --> 00:12:04.760
but rather, this was a
fear of the general public.

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:05.740
And what did it cause?

00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:07.140
It actually caused social death

00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:09.750
because you were completely
isolated from your family,

00:12:09.750 --> 00:12:11.450
you were isolated from your community,

00:12:11.450 --> 00:12:13.990
you were isolated from
any culture that you had,

00:12:13.990 --> 00:12:15.880
and you were sent there,
basically, to die.

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:17.900
And so, again, quarantines serve

00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:20.170
both political and social interests,

00:12:20.170 --> 00:12:22.020
but really, they never end up serving

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:25.240
those who need it the most,
which are usually the sick.

00:12:25.240 --> 00:12:29.293
- Wow, that's quite a striking
comment to end on there.

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:34.100
Well, maybe then, I'll
transition to a question

00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:36.520
that's in quite a different vein,

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:39.640
which is the CIFAR group
that you're a part of,

00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:41.723
the Humans and the Microbiome program,

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:45.180
is interested in how the
microbes that live in

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:47.993
and on us affect our
health and development.

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:53.300
We're often commenting
today on the negative impact

00:12:53.300 --> 00:12:57.370
that our overly or hyper
hygienic approaches

00:12:57.370 --> 00:13:00.490
are having on our microbial diversity.

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:05.490
What role has hygiene
played in past pandemics?

00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:09.290
- Yeah, I mean, that's a
really important question

00:13:09.290 --> 00:13:12.270
given the group that I'm a part of

00:13:12.270 --> 00:13:14.853
and fortunate to be a part
of, Human and the Microbiome.

00:13:14.853 --> 00:13:17.900
I mean, hygiene, if we
look at the next image,

00:13:17.900 --> 00:13:19.870
hygiene has a long, much longer history

00:13:19.870 --> 00:13:21.350
than most of us appreciate.

00:13:21.350 --> 00:13:22.930
The earliest accounts,

00:13:22.930 --> 00:13:24.748
and these are actually
written accounts, of course,

00:13:24.748 --> 00:13:27.550
they're the seventh century
Greek goddess Hygeia,

00:13:27.550 --> 00:13:29.770
who was also later a
Roman goddess as well,

00:13:29.770 --> 00:13:31.773
sanitation, cleanliness, and health.

00:13:33.180 --> 00:13:35.930
And so this is quite old,

00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:40.910
our appreciation of cleanliness
is next to godliness, right?

00:13:40.910 --> 00:13:43.360
And for the Romans, as shown
here in the illustration below,

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:45.040
it was all about controlling the water.

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:46.920
Water was personal hygiene,

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:49.540
so think about the Roman
baths, for instance,

00:13:49.540 --> 00:13:51.860
but it was also about
removal of fetid water

00:13:51.860 --> 00:13:53.330
and sewage and so on and so forth,

00:13:53.330 --> 00:13:55.520
so some of the earliest
sewers, of course, were Romans.

00:13:55.520 --> 00:13:57.380
They were exposed at some of the time,

00:13:57.380 --> 00:14:00.340
but still, it was the idea
was to get rid of waste.

00:14:00.340 --> 00:14:02.170
And I find it really interesting

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:04.070
because most of our sense of what we,

00:14:04.070 --> 00:14:06.820
when we think about the
Middle Ages and hygiene,

00:14:06.820 --> 00:14:09.440
we think of the Monty
Python's Holy Grail, right,

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:11.330
in which the guy dragging a cart

00:14:11.330 --> 00:14:12.570
with dead bodies through the town,

00:14:12.570 --> 00:14:14.210
and there's nothing but feces everywhere

00:14:14.210 --> 00:14:16.340
and it's dark and grim and muddy.

00:14:16.340 --> 00:14:19.530
And that's always our
perpetuated image of that,

00:14:19.530 --> 00:14:21.980
but we have evidence actually suggesting

00:14:21.980 --> 00:14:24.320
it really wasn't like
that in all situations.

00:14:24.320 --> 00:14:26.710
Yes, in impoverished parts of
the city, it might have been.

00:14:26.710 --> 00:14:28.150
In most cases, it really wasn't.

00:14:28.150 --> 00:14:29.560
People took much more care.

00:14:29.560 --> 00:14:34.380
So, I'd like say, we've
probably had this disconnect

00:14:34.380 --> 00:14:36.790
with our world since the rise of cities,

00:14:36.790 --> 00:14:38.980
and hygiene has probably
played a role in that

00:14:38.980 --> 00:14:42.320
diminution of that
microbiome over a long time.

00:14:42.320 --> 00:14:45.170
But really, today, there's
been an acceleration

00:14:45.170 --> 00:14:48.230
in that destruction of
our microbial constituents

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:52.770
in a hyper hygienic or
overly sanitized world,

00:14:52.770 --> 00:14:55.340
and that comes mostly because
of our fear of microbes right?

00:14:55.340 --> 00:14:57.840
I mean, all I have to do
is throw up some images

00:14:57.840 --> 00:14:59.880
of what plague was like
in the Middle Ages,

00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:04.100
and you talk about people,
30% of the population dying,

00:15:04.100 --> 00:15:06.110
makes good sense that people were

00:15:06.110 --> 00:15:10.040
really, really scared
about microbes, right?

00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:15.010
Microbes became the sort of,
the evil and feared thing,

00:15:15.010 --> 00:15:16.650
certainly when germ theory came about

00:15:16.650 --> 00:15:19.470
and we started identifying and
linking particular bacteria

00:15:19.470 --> 00:15:21.563
with known infectious diseases.

00:15:22.990 --> 00:15:26.480
And so I think because of
this over hyper sanitization

00:15:26.480 --> 00:15:28.360
that we go through now with Purell,

00:15:28.360 --> 00:15:31.593
I mean think about how important
Purell is currently, right,

00:15:33.090 --> 00:15:34.520
we've also, at the same time,

00:15:34.520 --> 00:15:36.950
we've sort of diminished
our microbial constituents,

00:15:36.950 --> 00:15:38.400
which are our first line of defense

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:39.711
both orally and in the gut

00:15:39.711 --> 00:15:42.683
as a way to defend
ourselves against infection.

00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:45.790
So, I think it's...

00:15:45.790 --> 00:15:48.600
Importantly, as people in the group,

00:15:48.600 --> 00:15:51.030
the Human and the Microbe
group have pointed out,

00:15:51.030 --> 00:15:53.370
there's a pendulum that
swings back and forth,

00:15:53.370 --> 00:15:57.880
and in the times of active
infectious disease and pandemics,

00:15:57.880 --> 00:15:59.710
hygiene is exceptionally important,

00:15:59.710 --> 00:16:01.310
washing your hands,

00:16:01.310 --> 00:16:03.440
making sure that if you come
into contact with somebody

00:16:03.440 --> 00:16:06.720
you wash your clothes,
you try to wear a mask,

00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:09.460
you try to clean things
that enter your house.

00:16:09.460 --> 00:16:12.730
So, that's an exceptionally
important point now,

00:16:12.730 --> 00:16:15.740
but the problem is how will we behave

00:16:15.740 --> 00:16:17.120
when the pandemic is over, right?

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:19.225
The behaviors during the Black Death,

00:16:19.225 --> 00:16:22.240
they continued for a long time thereafter.

00:16:22.240 --> 00:16:24.900
People had a hard time
switching back because,

00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:26.590
certainly in the case of Black Death,

00:16:26.590 --> 00:16:28.070
you had these repeated epidemics,

00:16:28.070 --> 00:16:31.160
so you were never really
without infectious disease.

00:16:31.160 --> 00:16:34.650
So, will we maintain
this hyper sterilization

00:16:34.650 --> 00:16:37.000
that we're going through now

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:39.060
and increase it, actually, more fold

00:16:39.060 --> 00:16:40.540
because of this epidemic,

00:16:40.540 --> 00:16:44.940
or will we switch back to
sort of a more healthier way,

00:16:44.940 --> 00:16:47.150
which is a return to nature

00:16:47.150 --> 00:16:50.513
rather than a continued
removal from nature?

00:16:51.760 --> 00:16:54.907
As our fearless leader
Brett Findlay would say,

00:16:54.907 --> 00:16:56.080
"Let them eat dirt," right?

00:16:56.080 --> 00:16:59.040
Return to the Earth so that
we can use those microbes

00:16:59.040 --> 00:17:02.490
as a way to stimulate our immune system

00:17:02.490 --> 00:17:04.810
in the event that that would
actually better protect us

00:17:04.810 --> 00:17:06.973
from the next coming pandemic.

00:17:07.840 --> 00:17:10.040
- That's interesting and
another question I have,

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:11.630
and I'm thinking about this also

00:17:11.630 --> 00:17:13.990
just looking at the backdrop
that you have there,

00:17:13.990 --> 00:17:17.650
the sort of dance macabre behind you,

00:17:17.650 --> 00:17:19.753
as you certainly know as
a scholar of pandemics,

00:17:19.753 --> 00:17:23.290
that it can often be quite a dark topic.

00:17:23.290 --> 00:17:26.910
But I'm wondering if it's
possible to also talk about

00:17:26.910 --> 00:17:29.190
how responses to certain epidemics

00:17:29.190 --> 00:17:32.950
have shown something
inspiring about humanity?

00:17:32.950 --> 00:17:34.820
- Yeah, yeah, I think
it's always important.

00:17:34.820 --> 00:17:37.800
I mean, pandemics have a
tendency to bring out the worst

00:17:37.800 --> 00:17:40.030
but then, also importantly,
bring out the best.

00:17:40.030 --> 00:17:43.620
I mean, I think there's a
balance that comes to the fear

00:17:43.620 --> 00:17:46.733
and loss in a sense of community,

00:17:47.880 --> 00:17:51.170
and it can strengthen
relationships between people.

00:17:51.170 --> 00:17:53.730
It can strengthen community relationships.

00:17:53.730 --> 00:17:57.890
It can strengthen your love
for particular individuals.

00:17:57.890 --> 00:17:59.860
During the Black Death,

00:17:59.860 --> 00:18:03.430
one of the famous engravings
is Death carrying off children

00:18:03.430 --> 00:18:05.090
because there was such
a high mortality rate

00:18:05.090 --> 00:18:06.290
of infants and children.

00:18:06.290 --> 00:18:10.013
And so thereafter, children
became much more important.

00:18:10.850 --> 00:18:11.870
Not that they weren't before,

00:18:11.870 --> 00:18:15.120
but there was this, much more
of a sense of preciousness

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:17.830
with children after the Black Death

00:18:17.830 --> 00:18:20.170
took so many of them away.

00:18:20.170 --> 00:18:22.900
And I think today about frontline workers.

00:18:22.900 --> 00:18:26.580
I also think about our elders
who we've, again, quarantined

00:18:26.580 --> 00:18:30.260
in essence, in a way.

00:18:30.260 --> 00:18:33.210
And I think of them in terms
of the sanatorium movement

00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:36.930
and how, will elders be more
precious in the years to come

00:18:36.930 --> 00:18:39.320
because they're now isolated?

00:18:39.320 --> 00:18:41.680
We can't actually have
direct contact with them.

00:18:41.680 --> 00:18:46.000
My parents are both
elderly, not yet in homes,

00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:47.850
but are being isolated themselves,

00:18:47.850 --> 00:18:49.920
and we can't see or speak

00:18:49.920 --> 00:18:52.910
and talk to them on a one on
one basis out of complete fear.

00:18:52.910 --> 00:18:56.317
And so I think it's
gonna change our behavior

00:18:56.317 --> 00:18:57.620
and our perspective on that.

00:18:57.620 --> 00:18:59.423
I think, importantly,

00:19:00.980 --> 00:19:04.090
I'm reminded every day of
the banging of the pots

00:19:04.090 --> 00:19:07.170
at seven o'clock for the
frontline workers in the ER

00:19:07.170 --> 00:19:08.810
and all those important things.

00:19:08.810 --> 00:19:11.020
I'm thinking about the Italians
singing on their balconies.

00:19:11.020 --> 00:19:15.070
And so I think even though
these are dark times

00:19:15.070 --> 00:19:16.510
and they certainly, in past epidemics,

00:19:16.510 --> 00:19:18.900
have brought exceptionally dark times,

00:19:18.900 --> 00:19:22.060
I think there's a tremendous
balance that comes with

00:19:23.168 --> 00:19:26.080
some of the beauty that
comes out of individuals

00:19:26.080 --> 00:19:30.110
making tremendous signs
of appreciation and love,

00:19:30.110 --> 00:19:32.590
and that's what can help us carry us

00:19:32.590 --> 00:19:34.673
through these challenging times.

00:19:35.700 --> 00:19:38.130
- Wow, well, thank you so much, Hendrik,

00:19:38.130 --> 00:19:40.910
for sharing your insights with us.

00:19:40.910 --> 00:19:43.980
Perhaps now I'm gonna turn
to some of the questions

00:19:43.980 --> 00:19:45.823
that have come in from our audience.

00:19:46.890 --> 00:19:51.640
So, one that's come in is
why do so many diseases

00:19:51.640 --> 00:19:54.843
leading to pandemics seem
to come from animals?

00:19:55.860 --> 00:19:58.670
- Yeah, well, I mean,
most of the diseases,

00:19:58.670 --> 00:20:00.508
the infectious diseases,

00:20:00.508 --> 00:20:03.760
well, about 60 to 70% of
them are zoonotic in origin.

00:20:03.760 --> 00:20:06.713
That is they jump from animal
reservoirs into humans.

00:20:07.780 --> 00:20:10.310
In the case of plague, of
course, these were rodents.

00:20:10.310 --> 00:20:13.670
Rodents are known to
harbor Yersinia pestis,

00:20:13.670 --> 00:20:17.340
and they jumped, unfortunately,
by spillover events

00:20:17.340 --> 00:20:20.720
that come from rodent
infections or rodent epidemics

00:20:20.720 --> 00:20:22.000
that then spill into humans.

00:20:22.000 --> 00:20:24.450
And these are just,
unfortunately, accidental

00:20:24.450 --> 00:20:26.690
emergences that happen

00:20:26.690 --> 00:20:28.850
when humans come into
close proximity, right?

00:20:28.850 --> 00:20:31.290
So, you think about humans
during the medieval ages

00:20:31.290 --> 00:20:32.610
and the populations are growing

00:20:32.610 --> 00:20:34.860
and all of a sudden you have an expansion

00:20:34.860 --> 00:20:39.350
into a more natural land.

00:20:39.350 --> 00:20:40.980
Maybe you're farming that land

00:20:40.980 --> 00:20:43.250
and so now you come into
contact with these rodents

00:20:43.250 --> 00:20:47.280
that then harbor these pathogens,

00:20:47.280 --> 00:20:49.610
which are in some cases not infectious

00:20:49.610 --> 00:20:51.490
to the rodent themselves.

00:20:51.490 --> 00:20:53.800
And so then they spill
accidentally into humans.

00:20:53.800 --> 00:20:55.730
And we know that that's the same case,

00:20:55.730 --> 00:20:57.430
of course, with COVID.

00:20:57.430 --> 00:21:00.190
These are bat origin, most likely,

00:21:00.190 --> 00:21:02.960
but it seems like they
can infect a wide host

00:21:02.960 --> 00:21:05.110
of other mammalian animals.

00:21:05.110 --> 00:21:07.812
So, there's talk of
course of the pangolins,

00:21:07.812 --> 00:21:10.060
so there seems to be a really
interesting evolutionary link

00:21:10.060 --> 00:21:12.340
between a pangolin and a bat,

00:21:12.340 --> 00:21:14.470
and now they're talking
about, of course, ferrets

00:21:14.470 --> 00:21:17.760
and other felines and so on and so forth.

00:21:17.760 --> 00:21:20.420
So, a large percentage of
these infectious agents

00:21:20.420 --> 00:21:21.253
are still out there.

00:21:21.253 --> 00:21:23.923
In fact, I mean, I think the
latest estimates are about

00:21:23.923 --> 00:21:28.230
2% of the known variome has
actually been sequenced,

00:21:28.230 --> 00:21:31.320
so there's a lot of viruses,
RNA viruses out there

00:21:31.320 --> 00:21:32.910
that are sitting in hosts.

00:21:32.910 --> 00:21:34.090
And, of course, as we continue

00:21:34.090 --> 00:21:36.300
and encroach on these landscapes,

00:21:36.300 --> 00:21:39.333
we're gonna run into these
spillover events repeatedly.

00:21:40.880 --> 00:21:43.110
- Another question that
kinda picks up on that point

00:21:43.110 --> 00:21:45.860
that's come in is how,
then, do we convince people

00:21:45.860 --> 00:21:48.283
that not all microbes are bad?

00:21:50.150 --> 00:21:55.120
- Well, I think the group
that I'm lucky to be a part of

00:21:55.120 --> 00:21:56.473
is one way to do that.

00:21:56.473 --> 00:21:58.930
I mean, we have to educate the young.

00:21:58.930 --> 00:22:00.440
And I see it happening all the time.

00:22:00.440 --> 00:22:03.120
We talk about the important
bacteria in yogurt,

00:22:03.120 --> 00:22:05.410
the important bacteria in cheese and milk

00:22:05.410 --> 00:22:08.950
and how eating healthy vegetables can add

00:22:08.950 --> 00:22:10.710
important fiber to your gut

00:22:10.710 --> 00:22:12.970
and your gut contains all these microbes,

00:22:12.970 --> 00:22:14.440
and so I think we need to,

00:22:14.440 --> 00:22:16.279
we've really talked
about individual bacteria

00:22:16.279 --> 00:22:18.220
over the course of history
that have had massive

00:22:18.220 --> 00:22:21.680
effects on mortality rates,

00:22:21.680 --> 00:22:23.270
but we need to talk in the same way

00:22:23.270 --> 00:22:25.180
about all these great bacteria,

00:22:25.180 --> 00:22:27.110
the lactobacillus, for example,

00:22:27.110 --> 00:22:29.670
that are important bacteria from milk,

00:22:29.670 --> 00:22:32.830
that are dairy-based products
that play a tremendous role

00:22:32.830 --> 00:22:35.900
in providing important neurotransmitters

00:22:35.900 --> 00:22:38.280
and communicate back and
forth with our brains

00:22:38.280 --> 00:22:40.940
and can modulate depression and anxiety

00:22:40.940 --> 00:22:43.180
and in some cases even obesity.

00:22:43.180 --> 00:22:44.700
So, there's a tremendous,

00:22:44.700 --> 00:22:48.530
I think the more we sort
of open up this fantastic

00:22:48.530 --> 00:22:52.350
microbial box that is our system,

00:22:52.350 --> 00:22:55.230
our holobiont as it's placed,

00:22:55.230 --> 00:22:57.700
that we are not just human cells.

00:22:57.700 --> 00:22:59.550
We're sort of more bacterial and viral

00:22:59.550 --> 00:23:01.170
and fungal cells than we are human,

00:23:01.170 --> 00:23:03.390
which is an interesting
way to think about it.

00:23:03.390 --> 00:23:06.500
And as that becomes increasingly
more and better known,

00:23:06.500 --> 00:23:08.750
I think people will really appreciate

00:23:08.750 --> 00:23:10.710
there's a fine balance between those

00:23:10.710 --> 00:23:14.450
that are really problematic,
which it's really a handful.

00:23:14.450 --> 00:23:17.370
It's a very small number
of infectious agents so far

00:23:17.370 --> 00:23:20.270
that have really caused
the kind of epidemics

00:23:20.270 --> 00:23:22.910
that I sort of study in the past.

00:23:22.910 --> 00:23:26.260
The bulk of them do very little
to us if anything at all,

00:23:26.260 --> 00:23:29.560
and a huge host of them that we live with

00:23:29.560 --> 00:23:34.560
are synthons, helpful, (mumbles)
that help us digest foods

00:23:34.850 --> 00:23:38.100
and fight infection
and so on and so forth.

00:23:38.100 --> 00:23:40.360
- That's terrific there.

00:23:40.360 --> 00:23:43.550
I'm sort of almost distracted
by all these questions.

00:23:43.550 --> 00:23:46.610
I think this conversation could
go on for quite a long time.

00:23:46.610 --> 00:23:49.050
I'm having to choose amongst
terrific questions here,

00:23:49.050 --> 00:23:52.730
but maybe I'll ask one of the
ones that just just came in.

00:23:52.730 --> 00:23:56.040
But it's, the question
is that it seems that

00:23:56.040 --> 00:23:58.910
with most plagues there
are great inequalities

00:23:58.910 --> 00:24:00.280
in how they affect different groups

00:24:00.280 --> 00:24:02.660
and populations in society.

00:24:02.660 --> 00:24:04.720
Can you think of examples where societies

00:24:04.720 --> 00:24:06.883
have handled these
diseases more equitably?

00:24:08.180 --> 00:24:09.983
- Oh, that's a very good question.

00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:14.780
Epidemics and pandemics
are an immediate window

00:24:14.780 --> 00:24:17.890
onto the state of affairs
of any particular country,

00:24:17.890 --> 00:24:21.140
county, nation, however
you wanna divide it,

00:24:21.140 --> 00:24:24.200
and they show, they immediately
bring to the surface

00:24:24.200 --> 00:24:26.703
the inequity that exists within societies.

00:24:29.100 --> 00:24:31.400
So, your question was is there,

00:24:31.400 --> 00:24:34.100
can I think of a case where
they dealt more equitably?

00:24:34.970 --> 00:24:37.640
That's a really, really good question.

00:24:37.640 --> 00:24:40.400
Off the top of my head, unfortunately, no.

00:24:40.400 --> 00:24:42.020
I mean, the one thing epidemics do

00:24:42.020 --> 00:24:45.590
is they immediately highlight
government failures, right?

00:24:45.590 --> 00:24:49.120
And so in any case where you have

00:24:50.272 --> 00:24:53.820
dramatic inequality of
wealth, for example,

00:24:53.820 --> 00:24:56.260
pandemics always, always effect

00:24:57.370 --> 00:25:00.210
those of lower socioeconomic income

00:25:00.210 --> 00:25:03.220
much harder than they do the other ones,

00:25:03.220 --> 00:25:05.540
those that aren't in those situations,

00:25:05.540 --> 00:25:09.990
because they don't have access
to proper food and nutrition

00:25:09.990 --> 00:25:11.330
and so their immunocompromised.

00:25:11.330 --> 00:25:12.850
They don't have access to clean water.

00:25:12.850 --> 00:25:14.390
We know that's a big problem today

00:25:14.390 --> 00:25:15.820
across even our country, right?

00:25:15.820 --> 00:25:17.900
The indigenous populations in the north,

00:25:17.900 --> 00:25:20.350
they don't have access to
proper healthcare and medicines,

00:25:20.350 --> 00:25:23.770
so this is a question I'll
take with me after this

00:25:23.770 --> 00:25:24.880
to really think hard about

00:25:24.880 --> 00:25:27.020
because it certainly doesn't
come to mind immediately.

00:25:27.020 --> 00:25:29.080
Most of the cases that I
can think about of the past

00:25:29.080 --> 00:25:31.760
always shows dramatic inequity

00:25:31.760 --> 00:25:34.663
across populations unfortunately, sadly.

00:25:35.870 --> 00:25:38.800
And it's still true today
in the current epidemic,

00:25:38.800 --> 00:25:41.843
again highlighting all these
social issues once again.

00:25:42.720 --> 00:25:44.510
- Thank you for that response.

00:25:44.510 --> 00:25:46.640
Unfortunately, we've only
got about two minutes left,

00:25:46.640 --> 00:25:49.010
so I might try to squeeze
in one last question

00:25:49.010 --> 00:25:49.940
if you're interested,

00:25:49.940 --> 00:25:53.270
and it relates more to your own research,

00:25:53.270 --> 00:25:57.210
which involves recovering
DNA from centuries ago.

00:25:57.210 --> 00:26:01.230
And someone is asking what
those ancient DNA tell us

00:26:01.230 --> 00:26:03.773
about ancient pathogens and pandemics.

00:26:04.760 --> 00:26:07.283
- Yeah, that's very, very good questions.

00:26:09.720 --> 00:26:11.927
Well, so I think one of
the important things that,

00:26:11.927 --> 00:26:15.060
well, one of the first thing
I think is really important

00:26:15.060 --> 00:26:18.400
is they can help really
identify the etiology

00:26:18.400 --> 00:26:22.080
of a particular infectious epidemic.

00:26:22.080 --> 00:26:23.470
And that might seem like,

00:26:23.470 --> 00:26:26.650
well, that's sort of an easy answer,

00:26:26.650 --> 00:26:29.420
but you have to understand
that in the absence,

00:26:29.420 --> 00:26:33.060
in a vacuum of an identifiable agent,

00:26:33.060 --> 00:26:35.500
blame can go in all sorts
of different directions.

00:26:35.500 --> 00:26:38.040
So, remember, during the Black Death,

00:26:38.040 --> 00:26:40.710
during that absence of
knowledge of what that was,

00:26:40.710 --> 00:26:42.620
people of course blamed everybody

00:26:42.620 --> 00:26:44.060
that you can imagine under the sun,

00:26:44.060 --> 00:26:45.510
so prostitutes, Jews.

00:26:45.510 --> 00:26:47.200
Everybody was either burned at the stake

00:26:47.200 --> 00:26:49.300
or tortured in various ways

00:26:49.300 --> 00:26:53.372
that we don't even want
to get into or talk about.

00:26:53.372 --> 00:26:56.047
And that still, you would
think, "Oh, well, Hendrik,

00:26:56.047 --> 00:26:57.160
"that's something of the past."

00:26:57.160 --> 00:26:58.730
Well, no, think about HIV, right?

00:26:58.730 --> 00:27:00.090
For the longest time,

00:27:00.090 --> 00:27:05.090
the HIV wasn't even associated
as being a potential virus,

00:27:05.120 --> 00:27:06.670
and because it happened to be

00:27:06.670 --> 00:27:10.060
in a disenfranchised community,
in the homosexual community

00:27:10.910 --> 00:27:12.970
in San Francisco and
New York and Montreal,

00:27:12.970 --> 00:27:15.070
it was really stigmatized

00:27:15.070 --> 00:27:17.600
as sort of unimportant and insignificant.

00:27:17.600 --> 00:27:19.410
And when that identity became aware,

00:27:19.410 --> 00:27:20.720
it was really significant.

00:27:20.720 --> 00:27:21.810
So, I think one of the things

00:27:21.810 --> 00:27:23.980
is really that immediate identity

00:27:23.980 --> 00:27:26.600
of what those infectious
agents were in the past.

00:27:26.600 --> 00:27:29.100
The second thing, of
course, is do we understand

00:27:29.100 --> 00:27:30.890
if its behavior in the past is different

00:27:30.890 --> 00:27:32.630
to how it might act still today

00:27:32.630 --> 00:27:35.960
or if re-emergences continue to happen.

00:27:35.960 --> 00:27:38.530
How can we actually modulate our behavior

00:27:38.530 --> 00:27:40.330
differently depending upon that?

00:27:40.330 --> 00:27:43.300
Are there things that we
can do to actually contact

00:27:43.300 --> 00:27:47.020
and trace and be aware,
screen, for example,

00:27:47.020 --> 00:27:49.490
if we know the evolutionary
history of that?

00:27:49.490 --> 00:27:50.330
The third thing, of course,

00:27:50.330 --> 00:27:52.720
is it talks about an
evolutionary descendant ratio

00:27:52.720 --> 00:27:55.080
between the ancient infectious agents

00:27:55.080 --> 00:27:56.420
and the contemporary ones,

00:27:56.420 --> 00:27:58.280
and I think that plays
also an important role

00:27:58.280 --> 00:28:00.720
in understanding its
evolutionary trajectory.

00:28:00.720 --> 00:28:02.370
Where is it going from here?

00:28:02.370 --> 00:28:03.483
Where has it been?

00:28:04.523 --> 00:28:05.810
I think we're running out of time,

00:28:05.810 --> 00:28:09.277
so I could go on and on about this topic.

00:28:09.277 --> 00:28:10.110
- I'm sure you could.

00:28:10.110 --> 00:28:12.730
That's been really,
really fantastic, Hendrik.

00:28:12.730 --> 00:28:13.870
Thank you so much.

00:28:13.870 --> 00:28:16.730
And I'd also really like to
thank everyone for joining in

00:28:16.730 --> 00:28:19.663
and giving use such terrific
questions to feed you.

00:28:20.830 --> 00:28:25.830
So, for everyone, please
visit our website, cifar.ca,

00:28:25.950 --> 00:28:27.620
to find out more about this series.

00:28:27.620 --> 00:28:29.250
We'll have a few more talks coming up,

00:28:29.250 --> 00:28:32.463
and we also have previous
recorded talks available there.

00:28:33.360 --> 00:28:34.410
But yes, just in signing off,

00:28:34.410 --> 00:28:36.110
I'd like to thank you again, Hendrik,

00:28:36.110 --> 00:28:39.860
and wish you all a great afternoon.

00:28:39.860 --> 00:28:41.265
- [Hendrik] Well, thank you.

00:28:41.265 --> 00:28:42.265
- Bye.
- Bye.

